<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

  <!--
    The dark palette: the Agent App's look since the CallPoc proof of concept,
    a dark shell, one blue accent, and cards on a near-black canvas. The one
    an agent starts in (Dia, 4 Oct 2026); Themes\Light.xaml is the other, and
    ThemeService swaps one for the other (A-90).

    #4F8CFF started as a placeholder carried over from that proof of concept.
    On 2026-09-20 the client chose to keep it, so it is the brand colour rather
    than a stand-in. The supervisor app's brand.400/500/600 is the same value -
    change both together or the two apps stop matching.

    Dark suits the job. An agent has this on screen for an eight-hour shift,
    often in a back room, and a white full-screen window for that long is
    tiring. It also lets the one accent colour carry meaning - what is selected,
    what is the primary action - instead of competing with a bright background.

    Every key here is in Light.xaml too (ThemeResourcesTests), and every use
    of one is a DynamicResource, so a swap reaches what is already on screen.
  -->

  <Color x:Key="BgColor">#0F1115</Color>
  <Color x:Key="AccentColor">#4F8CFF</Color>

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Bg" Color="#0F1115" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Surface" Color="#171A21" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceAlt" Color="#1E222B" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BorderSoft" Color="#2A2F3A" />

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Text" Color="#E6E8EC" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextMuted" Color="#8B93A7" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextFaint" Color="#5E667A" />

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Accent" Color="#4F8CFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentHover" Color="#6BA0FF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentDim" Color="#25355A" />

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Success" Color="#3FB950" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Warning" Color="#D29922" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Danger" Color="#F85149" />

  <!-- Text on a solid Success background: the green Answer button. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="OnSuccess" Color="#08130A" />

  <!-- A box that needs the agent's attention without being an error: the
       warning's colour, dark, as a background, with WarningText on it. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WarningDim" Color="#2A2412" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WarningText" Color="#E8C56B" />
  <!-- Text on a solid Warning background: the VIP badge, a count badge. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="OnWarning" Color="#1A1200" />

  <!-- The chips and boxes that used to carry these colours inline (review,
       27 Sep): same colours, now named once here. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WarningChip" Color="#3A2E12" />   <!-- VIP chip in the contacts list -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DangerChip" Color="#3B1618" />    <!-- Blocked chip -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DangerDim" Color="#2A1416" />     <!-- an error box: the sign-in refusal -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DangerText" Color="#FF9B95" />    <!-- text in an error box -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SuccessDim" Color="#14261B" />    <!-- "Classified" chip -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BadgeBg" Color="#1F2A37" />       <!-- the pop-up's queue and direction badges -->

</ResourceDictionary>
